# The explicit deployment trigger for l5-halt-watchdog.
#
# `watchPatterns` on this service is exactly
# ["deploy/l5_halt_watchdog/DEPLOY_TRIGGER"], so pushing code to the branch
# does NOT deploy it. Changing this file does.
#
# WHY THE WATCHDOG NEEDS THIS AND NOT JUST THE EXECUTOR. The executor has
# been isolated behind its own trigger for a while; the watchdog was not
# isolated at all, so every ordinary push redeployed BOTH. The point of an
# independent watchdog is that it is still watching when the executor is
# restarted, crashed or wedged -- and a supervisor that restarts in the same
# second as the process it supervises provides none of that. Measured on
# 2026-10-15: executor deployment 4f3d3df6 and watchdog deployment ce8e2b6e
# were both created at 12:13:04Z from commit 669fb3ac, and the previous
# watchdog instance was removed at 12:14:27Z.
#
# THE LATCH IS NOT IN THIS IMAGE. It lives on the volume at /data, so it
# survives every deployment here. A deploy that fails leaves the executor
# unable to read the latch, which `l5_halt_gate` treats as
# `halt_state_unavailable` and therefore HALTED -- the safe direction.
#
# Nothing here arms anything, clears a halt, or changes a capital limit.
pin watchdog deploy isolation, shadow-locked 2026-10-15T13:20Z

# 2026-10-17T21:06Z: owner-authorised TEMPORARY swap to the one-shot
# deploy/l5_halt_reset image to run the READ-ONLY HALT_RESET_INSPECT dry run
# and nothing else. L5_HALT_RESET_INSPECT is pinned to YES on the service, so
# main() takes the inspect branch and returns before perform_reset is
# reachable. NO RESET. The watchdog image, volume, branch, builder,
# watchPatterns and every other variable are untouched, and the three changed
# fields (dockerfilePath, startCommand, restartPolicyType) are restored to
# deploy/l5_halt_watchdog/Dockerfile, python3 -m scripts.l5_halt_watchdog and
# ALWAYS immediately afterwards.
inspect the latched halt, read-only, owner-authorised 2026-10-17T21:06Z

